Let's find this limit directly.

To do that, we will want to divide both the numerator and the denominator by the same quantity, in a way that will help us derive the limit.

Since the leading term of the numerator is $x$, let's divide by $x$.

In the denominator, let's divide by $\sqrt{x^2}$, since for positive values, $x=\sqrt{x^2}$.

$\begin{align}
&\phantom{=}\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{3x-1}{\sqrt{x^2-6}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{3x-1}{x}}{\dfrac{\sqrt{x^2-6}}{\sqrt{x^2}}}\qquad\gray{\text{Divide sides by }x=\sqrt{x^2}}
\end{align}$

Now let's continue by simplifying the expression and using the fact that for any nonzero number $k$ and positive power $n$, the limit $\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}$ is equal to $0$.

$\begin{align}
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{\dfrac{3\cancel{x}}{\cancel{x}}-\dfrac{1}{x}}{\sqrt{\dfrac{1\cancel{x^2}}{\cancel{x^2}}-\dfrac{6}{ x^2}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{3-\dfrac{1}{x}}{\sqrt{1-\dfrac{6}{x^2}}}
\\\\
&=\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{3-0}{\sqrt{1-0}}\qquad\gray{\displaystyle\lim_{x\to\infty}\dfrac{k}{x^n}=0}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{3}{\sqrt{1}}
\\\\
&=\dfrac{3}{1}
\\\\
&=3
\end{align}$
